<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Perak Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1528 CE – present · capital Kuala Kangsar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Malay sultanate founded by a son of Malacca's last sultan, grown rich on tin, and still reigning as a state of Malaysia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> fell to Portugal in 1511, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-of-malacca">Sultan Mahmud Shah</a>'s eldest son, Raja Muzaffar, was invited by the people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perak-river">Perak River</a> valley to be their ruler. He was installed around 1528 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-shah-i">Muzaffar Shah I</a>, founding a dynasty that still reigns.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Perak's wealth came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, mined in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinta-valley">Kinta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larut">Larut</a> valleys. In the 17th century it fell under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate</a> and later signed tin contracts with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>. Its succession system rotated the throne among branches of the royal family, and the court settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuala-kangsar">Kuala Kangsar</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Disputes over succession and violent rivalry among Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> miners led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pangkor-treaty-of-1874">Pangkor Treaty of 1874</a>, which brought a British Resident. The killing of the Resident J. W. W. Birch in 1875 led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perak-war">Perak War</a>. Perak joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federated-malay-states">Federated Malay States</a> in 1895 and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia</a> in 1963. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazrin-shah-of-perak">Sultan Nazrin Shah</a> reigns today.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/perak-sultanate.jpg" alt="Perak Sultanate" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Aimanrasul · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Standard_of_the_Sultan_of_Perak.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1528 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kuala Kangsar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muzaffar Shah I, Abdullah Muhammad Shah II, Nazrin Shah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Perak, Perak</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1777&amp;focus=perak-sultanate" aria-label="Perak Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7479 1999 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Perak Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7799 2255L7839 2352M7799 2255L7642 2226M7799 2255L4997 651M7799 2255L7823 2316M7799 2255L7823 2316M7799 2255L7823 2316"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7839 2352h0M7642 2226h0M4997 651h0M7823 2316h0M7823 2316h0M7823 2316h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7799 2255h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7799 2255h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kuala Kangsar. <a href="/atlas?year=1777&amp;focus=perak-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
